            "body": "Hi @bIackr0se, thanks for picking up #42528 (I filed that issue). FYI there is a second PR for the same bug, #42850, which takes a different route, so the maintainers may want to look at both together.\n\nDifference in approach:\n- #42534 (this PR): remember the last rejected candidate with positive target mass at the node and emit it when the residual is empty.\n- #42850: when the residual `(p - q)+` is empty, fall back to sampling from the target distribution `p` itself.\n\nMy reasoning for the target-sampling fallback: the empty residual can only arise from float rounding (in exact arithmetic the residual always has mass when the draft is rejected), so the fallback should keep the emitted token distributed like the target. Sampling from `p` does that for any shape of `p`; returning one remembered candidate is equivalent in the single-token-nucleus case from the issue, but when several candidates carry mass it deterministically favours one of them, and it needs extra per-node state in the kernel. #42850 also keeps the kernel change small and includes the reproduction from #42528 as a unit test.\n\nHappy to fold your coin-table test into #42850 (or the other way round) — whichever the maintainers prefer. Both PRs are currently waiting on the `run-ci` label.",
            "createdAt": "2026-10-08T09:33:40Z",
            "databaseId": 6056967267,
            "updatedAt": "2026-10-08T09:33:40Z",
            "url": "https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang/pull/42534#issuecomment-6056967267"
          },
          {
            "author": {
              "__typename": "User",
              "login": "bIackr0se"
            },
            "body": "Thanks @clayboby, I agree that the empty residual comes from rounding and both fixes prevent the zero-probability token, with the difference confined to an event of roughly 2^-24 per node. The sibling loop uses one coin against cumulative target mass and copies each rejected sibling's mass into `draft_probs`, so an empty residual means that coin reached or exceeded the rounded total. My reason for retaining the last positive-mass candidate is that this coin falls in its interval in exact arithmetic: #42534 returns the token the CDF test would have selected without rounding. Your fallback in #42850 samples from `p` using the final-sampling coin, giving a target-distributed draw that can differ from the token selected by the original coin. The cost trade-off is one extra variable and no extra pass in #42534 versus a vocabulary-wide block reduction only on the rare empty path in #42850. I'm happy with whichever approach the maintainers prefer, and glad to fold my three coin-table cases (rounded-CDF equality, shortfall, and siblings exhausting support) into whichever lands.\n",
            "createdAt": "2026-10-19T10:11:28Z",
            "databaseId": 6264812262,
            "updatedAt": "2026-10-19T10:11:28Z",
